{:event/type "kanban.task-created", :event/id "agile-62b3d579-70a2-4670-9d7c-a23bc89b06f8-1791746994972", :event/time "2026-10-11T19:29:54.972Z", :session/id "agile", :delivery/mode "tell", :payload {:task-id "62b3d579-70a2-4670-9d7c-a23bc89b06f8", :dependency nil, :parent nil, :card-type "task", :type "task-created", :write-id "1791746994964-0.0bblyav5zqyxvw5wypl", :source "cli", :agent "eta-mu", :title "Distinct future native event identities", :source-path "/home/err/spaces/cephalon-rheos-event-epic/rheos/docs/agile/tasks/distinct-future-native-event-identities-c89b06f8.md", :status "incoming", :epic nil, :body "# Distinct future native event identities\n\n## Context\n\nThe native Muse planning edit emitted two frontmatter events at the same\nmillisecond on 2026-10-11. Both retained the event ID\n`kanban-bb5b1ee2-111c-4762-8321-889a7256b2f9-1791744352193`, while\ntheir title/labels payloads and causal/root identities differ. The owning\n`kanban-envelope` currently identifies events by board, task and `Date.now()`.\nMuse PR6 findings 4242786176 and 4242844100 identify the collision. The human\nincluded this narrow owning prerequisite in the accepted encounter/mood/recall\nmilestone. Existing receipts and events are immediately append-only.\n\n## Outcome\n\nEvery future native event emission receives an independently generated,\ncollision-resistant identity. Frozen or coarse wall-clock time cannot make\ndistinct emissions reuse an event ID. Original events remain inspectable.\n\n## Scope\n\nRepair identity construction at the shared owning emission chokepoint. Keep\nboard/task identity, event type, timestamp, payload, write correlation,\ncausal/root provenance, awaited persistence and notification ordering intact.\nUse a host identity primitive at the effect boundary; keep any reusable shape\nor decision portable. Exercise all existing native event families and the real\nmulti-field writer. Consumer correction uses a native comment referring to\nthe original collision and exact retained rows after qualified adoption.\n\n## Non-goals\n\nNo renumbering, deletion, deduplication or reordering of historical events;\nno ledger migration, new correction protocol, local consumer event writer,\nclock/status redesign, dependency upgrade, package release, paid review,\nruntime restart, deployment or claim that the character milestone is complete.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Distinct emissions for the same board/task/type remain distinct when\n  time is held constant, including multiple fields from one real writer batch.\n  Independent instances do not depend on a shared in-memory counter alone.\n- [ ] All native event families retain their exact event vocabulary, board,\n  task, timestamp, payload, source/agent and write correlation meanings. The\n  identifier remains an opaque string accepted by the existing admission and\n  event readers; no consumer parsing/migration is required.\n- [ ] Persistence is still awaited before publication. Refused writes and\n  publication/reservation failures retain their existing visible outcomes;\n  generating a nonce cannot turn an unpersisted event into a success.\n- [ ] Tests retain a seeded historical duplicate-ID prefix byte-for-byte.\n  New event appends have distinct IDs and remain independently readable.\n- [ ] After qualified source adoption, an actual native Muse corrective\n  comment identifies both original rows by ID, location and exact byte hashes.\n  Independent readback proves the comment and original prefix coexist. This\n  documents the historical collision without claiming the old IDs are fixed.\n\n## Verification\n\nPlanning qualification and native Ready precede implementation. Commit failing\ncompiled regressions against the qualified predecessor, then repair the owner.\nRun existing full lint/test/build gates and meaningful frozen-clock native\nwriter tests. Record exact source/CLI hashes, actual commands, independently\nread-back events and append-only prefix checks. Owned fixtures clean up in\nfinally. Source approval and real consumer adoption are separate evidence.\n\n## Risks\n\nRandom identities provide collision resistance, not a mathematical proof of\nglobal uniqueness. Time freezing must not accidentally alter UUID generation\nor skip real ledger admission. Historical duplicate IDs remain a known data\ndefect; corrective provenance must distinguish their different immutable rows.\n\nProcess documentation: GPL-3.0-or-later."}, :causal/root "c08eb6c1-eb7f-4599-a94c-b6a5a9f613a6"}
{:event/type "kanban.task-created", :event/id "agile-a6de84ce-06c4-4556-a9ba-6bbb78c7b60a-1791746995528", :event/time "2026-10-11T19:29:55.528Z", :session/id "agile", :delivery/mode "tell", :payload {:task-id "a6de84ce-06c4-4556-a9ba-6bbb78c7b60a", :dependency ["62b3d579-70a2-4670-9d7c-a23bc89b06f8"], :parent nil, :card-type "task", :type "task-created", :write-id "1791746995518-0.cpe1zck8g9r3wpskpm7", :source "cli", :agent "eta-mu", :title "Lawful incoming-card epic correction", :source-path "/home/err/spaces/cephalon-rheos-event-epic/rheos/docs/agile/tasks/lawful-incoming-card-epic-correction-78c7b60a.md", :status "incoming", :epic nil, :body "# Lawful incoming-card epic correction\n\n## Context\n\nMuse PR6 coordinator `bb5b1ee2-111c-4762-8321-889a7256b2f9` is Incoming\nand has epic category/labels, but lacks the actual `type: epic`. Two Incoming\nchildren name it as both parent and epic. Native `frontmatter --set type=epic`\nrefuses the protected/unknown key; removing only one child's epic also refuses\nbecause the complete proposed graph remains invalid. Findings 4242786186 and\nthe actual native refusal proof retain this distinction. The human included\nthe narrow owning repair; hand-editing operational type or filtering away the\nother child is not authorized.\n\n## Outcome\n\nThe existing canonical writer can correct an Incoming ordinary card to an\nactual epic when, and only when, the complete proposed graph is lawful. The\ncorrection retains UUID, status, source identity, body and prior provenance.\n\n## Scope\n\nAdd a portable conditional admission law for this one direction: a card whose\ncanonical current status is incoming and whose prior type is absent, task or\nstory may become exact epic. Route it through the existing frontmatter writer\nand its complete selected-board snapshot, reservation, revision recheck and\nfile/event publication boundary. Keep public CLI/HTTP/MCP diagnostics and\ndocumentation consistent; do not add an alternate operational command engine.\nAn already-epic Incoming card's identical request may be an admitted semantic\nno-op only after whole-graph validation, with no file/event effect.\n\n## Non-goals\n\nNo general type editor, epic downgrade, status transition/bypass, mutable UUID,\nidentity/provenance rewrite, inference from labels/category/title, partial graph,\nmanual live type patch, caller-local parser, new board engine, broad repair of\nother cards, release/deployment or character milestone completion.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] The pure law admits absent/task/story to exact epic only for an Incoming\n  subject and a valid complete post-update graph. It can repair existing\n  child epic references to that subject; it does not require the inherited\n  graph to be valid before the lawful correction is considered.\n- [ ] Other target types, malformed prior types, non-Incoming subjects,\n  unknown/ambiguous subjects, unrelated malformed cards, missing references,\n  cycles and conflicting memberships refuse before any write or event.\n  UUID/status/write-id/provenance updates remain protected, including mixed\n  batches. An epic downgrade is refused even if its proposed graph is valid.\n- [ ] The native complete-board writer preserves subject identity, status,\n  body/comments and unrelated card bytes. The successful event accurately\n  records the old/new type; all historical events retain their exact prefix.\n- [ ] Concurrent source change, publication reservation contention and\n  file/event failures retain the existing classified failure behavior. The\n  correction cannot reuse a stale graph decision or bypass a lower-level\n  writer guard. CLI, HTTP and MCP use that same canonical admission.\n- [ ] After current-head qualification and exact CLI adoption, the actual Muse\n  correction is performed natively. Independent readback proves exact epic,\n  both children's retained UUID relationships and scalar dependency, and an\n  unchanged original event prefix. Remaining Ready/FSM/review gates still apply.\n\n## Verification\n\nPlanning qualification and native Ready precede portable/native RED, then\ndomain/adapters GREEN. Exercise JVM and compiled CLJS laws and real guarded\nwriters on complete owned boards, with refusal/no-op byte checks and existing\nreservation/publication regressions retained. Run full lint/test/build gates.\nRecord source/build hashes, exact commands and independent native consumer\nreadback. No local fixture alone admits the real Muse board.\n\n## Risks\n\nAdding type to a general descriptive allowlist would accidentally open every\nstatus and direction; the conditional authority must stay separate. Snapshot\nstatus normalization must use the existing canonical loader while retaining\nstored bytes. The full graph can remain invalid for unrelated reasons, in which\ncase the real correction must truthfully refuse instead of widening this scope.\n\nProcess documentation: GPL-3.0-or-later."}, :causal/root "ec843fb7-29a4-43b3-9428-e0186b059322"}
